    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>A brown-black band in the right great toenail, about 5 mm wide (about a third of the nail), with irregular dark lines, first noticed about two months ago after a knock at football and not growing out with the nail; the pigment extends onto the skin of the proximal nail fold (Hutchinson sign). Suspected subungual melanoma; dermoscopy and a full-thickness nail matrix biopsy booked this week</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>L60.8</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to his documented toe lesion; superseded by the diagnosis at the surgical oncology consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Acral lentiginous melanoma of the nail unit (subungual) of the right great toe, on full-thickness nail matrix biopsy: Breslow thickness 0.8 mm, not ulcerated, mitotic rate 1 per mm2, no lymphovascular or perineural invasion; clinical stage IB (cT1b N0 M0); synthetic modern detail linked to his documented acral lentiginous melanoma</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C43.71</td><td>S — Synthetic thickness and stage; the site, the subtype, and the year are documented (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Lateral sprain of the right ankle (anterior talofibular ligament) from an inversion injury at football; Ottawa ankle rules negative, so no X-ray; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>S93.401A</td><td>S — Synthetic acute injury; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

