  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acral lentiginous melanoma of the right great toe, stage IA, after digit-sparing excision (C43.71)</content>. No evidence of recurrence at the graft, in the nodes, or elsewhere on the skin. He is doing his self-examination well. No scans or blood tests are needed. [S]<list><item>Next melanoma review in six months, then every 6 to 12 months for 5 years.</item><item>Yearly full skin examination by dermatology, booked for the spring.</item><item>Keep the monthly self-examination and graft photographs on tour; see a doctor promptly for any new dark band, spot, lump, or sore that does not heal.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Lateral sprain of the right ankle (S93.401A)</content>. Healed. [S]<list><item>Ankle support for football for two more months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Full skin and nail examination, including the scalp, palms, soles, between the toes, and all nails</td><td>At diagnosis, then at each surveillance visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suspicious lesion</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Graft and scar</td><td>Each surveillance visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Healed; no pigment or nodule</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination of the lymph node basins (right popliteal and inguinal) and the scar</td><td>At diagnosis, then at each surveillance visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging (CT, PET, or ultrasound) and LDH</td><td>Not routine for stage I to II without symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stage IA without symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin self-examination</td><td>Monthly, taught after diagnosis and reviewed each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Done monthly with graft photographs; technique reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Dermatology full skin examination</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>Booked for the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain score</td><td>Each visit after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No pain</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>At each phase of care</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Tissue diagnosis within two weeks of presentation. Outcome by 1977-06-17: Dermoscopy and nail matrix biopsy two days after presentation; diagnosis explained on the ninth day [S]</item><item>Definitive surgery with clear margins and lymph node staging within four weeks of diagnosis, by the approach he chose. Outcome by 1977-07-15: Digit-sparing excision of the nail unit with clear margins and a negative sentinel node two weeks after diagnosis [S]</item><item>Walk, run, and play football normally within four months of surgery. Outcome by 1977-10-19: Running and playing football again; an ankle sprain at football healed in four weeks [S]</item><item>Monthly skin and nail self-examination, including the palms, soles, between the toes and all nails. Outcome by 1977-10-19: Taught after surgery; he has done it each month and keeps photographs of the graft [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Hepatitis B vaccine (HepB-CpG), dose 2 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Melanoma: Cutaneous, Version 3.2026 (2026); Swetter SM, et al. Guidelines of care for the management of primary cutaneous melanoma. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019;80(1):208-250 (2019); Chouquet L, et al. Reconstruction After Wide Excision of the Nail Apparatus in the Treatment of Melanoma: A Systematic Literature Review. J Clin Med 2025;14(17):5932 (2025); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Second hepatitis B vaccine dose given today, completing the series. Melanoma review in six months, or sooner for any new change. Dermatology in the spring. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
